Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Find the index of the second adjective (the one after the noun)
  let adjectiveIndex = -1;
  for (let i = words.length - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (/[a-zA-Z]+/.test(words[i])) {
      adjectiveIndex = i;
      break;
    }
  }

  // If we couldn't find two adjectives, return the original sentence
  if (adjectiveIndex === -1 || adjectiveIndex === 0) {
    return sentence;
  }

  // Swap the positions of the first and second adjectives
  const temp = words[adjectiveIndex];
  words[adjectiveIndex] = words[adjectiveIndex - 1];
  words[adjectiveIndex - 1] = temp;

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  return words.join(" ");
}

// Test the function with your example sentence
const exampleSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const result = switchAdjectives(exampleSentence);
console.log(result); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function uses a regex pattern to identify words that contain only letters (which should be our adjectives), and then finds the index of the second such word from the end of the array. It then swaps the positions of the first and second adjectives, and returns the modified array as a string. Note that this implementation assumes that there will always be at least two adjectives in the sentence, which may not be true in all cases. If you want to handle those situations differently, you can modify the function accordingly.

