Here's a JavaScript function that swaps the positions of adjacent words in a sentence based on their parts-of-speech (POS) tags, specifically identifying adjectives:

```javascript
function swapAdjacentWords(sentence) {
  const posTagger = require('pos');

  // Create an instance of the POS tagger
  const pos = new posTagger();

  // Split the sentence into an array of words and their corresponding POS tags
  const wordsAndTags = sentence.split(' ').map((word) => {
    return pos.tag(word);
  });

  // Swap adjacent words that are both adjectives
  for (let I = 0; I < wordsAndTags.length - 1; i++) {
    const currentWord = wordsAndTags[i];
    const nextWord = wordsAndTags[i+1];

    if (currentWord.includes('JJ') && nextWord.includes('JJ')) {
      [wordsAndTags[i], wordsAndTags[i+1]] = [wordsAndTags[i+1], wordsAndTags[i]];
    }
  }

  // Join the modified words and tags back into a sentence
  return wordsAndTags.map((wordAndTag) => {
    const [word, tag] = wordAndTag;
    return `${word} ${tag}`;
  }).join(' ');
}
```

The `posTagger` module is used to identify the parts-of-speech of each word in the sentence. We then iterate through the resulting array of words and their corresponding POS tags, swapping adjacent words that are both adjectives ('JJ'). Finally, we join the modified words and tags back together into a sentence and return it.

