To make the circle element in your CSS look more 3D, you can apply some additional CSS properties that create a 3D effect using perspective and transform. Here's an example:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray);
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
  width: 150px;
  height: 150px;
  position: relative;
}

.circle::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: -50px;
  left: -50px;
  right: -50px;
  bottom: -50px;
  z-index: -1;
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
  transform: rotateX(-30deg) translateZ(50px);
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  border-radius: 70%;
}

.circle:hover::before {
  transform: rotateX(-30deg) translateZ(100px);
}

body {
  perspective: 800px;
}
```

In this updated code snippet, we've added `perspective: 800px;` to the body element, which sets the perspective for the entire page. We've also added a pseudo-element (`::before`) with a transformed duplicate of the circle, which creates the illusion of depth. The `z-index` is set to -1 so it appears behind the original circle. When hovering over the circle, we apply a different `transform` to the pseudo-element, making it appear closer to the viewer. Feel free to adjust the values of the `perspective`, `transform`, and `transition` properties to achieve the desired 3D effect.

