response:
To further optimize the efficiency of finding unique words, we can use sparse matrices instead of sets for storing word counts. This allows us to perform set operations (like `setdiff`) on them without converting back to full matrices, which can be much faster for large input sizes. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1, text2)
    % Preprocess text data by converting to lowercase and splitting into words
    words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
    words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));

    % Count occurrences of each word in each text using sparse matrices
    count1 = sparse(size(words1));
    count2 = sparse(size(words2));
    for I = 1:size(words1, 1)
        count1(words1{i}) += 1;
    end
    for I = 1:size(words2, 1)
        count2(words2{i}) += 1;
    end

    % Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
    uniqueWords = sparse(count1) - sparse(count2);
    uniqueWords = unique(uniqueWords(:)); % Convert back to full matrix to extract unique elements
end

% Example usage:
% text1 = fileread('Text1.txt');
% text2 = fileread('Text2.txt');
% uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1, text2);
```

This implementation should have better performance for very large input sizes compared to using sets directly. The conversion back to full matrix at the end is necessary because `sparse(A)-sparse(B)` returns a sparse matrix containing only the non-zero differences between `A` and `B`, but we want all unique elements regardless of their frequency. By converting back to a full matrix, we ensure that all unique elements are included in the output.

