A classic example of a logical syllogism is Modus Ponens (MP), which is a valid argument form in classical logic. It can be expressed as follows:

\begin{tabular}{l|l}
Premise 1 (\P1): & \A \implies \B \\
Premise 2 (\P2): & \B \\
Conclusion (\C): & \A \\
\end{tabular}

The symbol $\implies$ represents the material conditional, which is true if either the antecedent (\A) is false or the consequent (\B) is true. In other words, $\A \implies \B$ is equivalent to $\neg \A \lor \B$.

To understand how MP works, let's break it down into steps.

1. Start with the two premises:

   \begin{tabular}{l|l}
   \P1: & \A \implies \B \\
   \P2: & \B \\
   \end{tabular}

2. Use the second premise (\P2) to substitute the consequent (\B) for the antecedent (\A) in the first premise (\P1). This creates a new statement called the subconclusion (\S):

   \begin{tabular}{l|l}
   \P1: & \A \implies \B \\
   \P2: & \B \\
   Subconclusion (\S): & \B \implies \B \\
   \end{tabular}

3. The subconclusion (\S) is equivalent to the identity statement (\I) because both sides of the implication (\B \implies \B) are the same. Therefore, we can replace the subconclusion (\S) with the identity statement (\I):

