To configure the boilerplate files of your Chrome extension to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, follow these steps:

1. Create a new Chrome extension project in your preferred development environment (such as Visual Studio Code or IntelliJ IDEA).
2. Add a manifest.json file to your project with the following content:

```json
{
  "name": "My Extension",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Injects JavaScript into webpages.",
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "tabs"],
  "browser_action": {
    "default_title": "Inject Script"
  },
  "background": {
    "scripts": ["background.js"]
  }
}
```

3. Create a background.js file with the following content:

```javascript
// Listen for the browser action button click event
browser.browserAction.onClicked.addListener(injectScript);

function injectScript() {
  // Get the current active tab
  browser.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, function(tabs) {
    // Inject the script into the content script of the current active tab
    browser.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, { file: "content.js" });
  });
}
```

