```jsx
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    let distance = Infinity;

    for (let I = 0; I < planets.length; i++) {
      const dist = getDistance(planet.position, planets[i].position);
      distance = Math.min(distance, dist);
    }

    if (distance >= minDistance) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};

function getDistance(a, b) {
  const dx = a[0] - b[0];
  const dy = a[1] - b[1];
  const dz = a[2] - b[2];

  return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz);
}
```

In this modified version, we keep track of the smallest distance between the current planet and all existing ones (stored in the `distance` variable). If this distance is greater than or equal to the minimum distance required, then the new planet is added to the list. Otherwise, we try again.

The `getDistance` helper function calculates the distance between two points using the formula for Euclidean distance in three dimensions.

