```javascript
const { promisify } = require('util');
const redis = require('redis');

class RedisClient {
  constructor() {
    this.client = new redis();

    // Handle errors
    this.client.on('error', err => {
      console.error('Redis Client Error:', err);
    });
  }

  async isAlive() {
    return await this.client.ping();
  }

  async get(key) {
    const result = await promisify(this.client.get.bind(this.client))(key);
    return result;
  }

  async set(key, value, ttl) {
    await promisify(this.client.setex.bind(this.client))(key, ttl, value);
  }

  async del(key) {
    await promisify(this.client.del.bind(this.client))(key);
  }
}

const redisClient = new RedisClient();
module.exports = redisClient;
```

I added `promisify` from `util` module to make all the Redis methods asynchronous and easier to use with `async/await`.

The `isAlive` method simply calls the `ping` method of the Redis client and returns its result.

All other methods are asynchronous and wrap their respective Redis commands using `bind` and `promisify`. This allows us to use them with `async/await` syntax.

Finally, I created an instance of the `RedisClient` class and exported it for use elsewhere in our application.

Here's an implementation of `RedisClient` as described:

```javascript
const redis = require('redis');
const { promisify } = require('util');

class RedisClient {
  constructor(host, port, password) {
    this.client = new redis.createClient({
      host,
      port,
      password,
      retry_strategy: (times) => {
        // Retry after 500ms up to 5 times
        return Math.min(times * 500, 5000);
      },
    });

    // Handle errors from Redis client
    this.client.on('error', (err) => {
      console.error(`Error: ${err}`);
    });
  }

  async isAlive() {
    try {
      await this.client.ping();
      return true;
    } catch (err) {
      return false;
    }
  }

  async get(key) {
    const value = await promisify(this.client.get.bind)(this.client)(key);
    return value;
  }

  async set(key, value, duration) {
    await promisify(this.client.setex.bind)(this.client)(key, duration, value);
  }

  async del(key) {
    await promisify(this.client.del.bind)(this.client)(key);
  }
}

// Create and export instance of RedisClient
const redisClient = new RedisClient('localhost', 6379);
module.exports = redisClient;
```

This implementation uses the `redis` package to interact with Redis, and adds some extra features such as handling errors, implementing `promisify` to make asynchronous methods more convenient, and providing an `isAlive` method to check if the Redis connection is still alive. The `duration` parameter for `set` is used to set an expiration time for the value in seconds.

Here's the implementation of `RedisClient` class in `redis.js`:

```javascript
const redis = require("redis");
const { promisify } = require("util");

class RedisClient {
  constructor() {
    this.client = new redis();
    this.client.on("connect", () => {
      console.log("Connected to Redis...");
    });
    this.client.on("error", (err) => {
      console.error("Error connecting to Redis:", err);
    });
  }

  isAlive() {
    return this.client.ping().then(() => true).catch(() => false);
  }

  get(key) {
    return promisify(this.client.get)(key);
  }

  set(key, value, ttl) {
    return promisify(this.client.setex)(key, ttl, value);
  }

  del(key) {
    return promisify(this.client.del)(key);
  }
}

const redisClient = new RedisClient();
module.exports = redisClient;
```

In this implementation, we first import required modules - `redis` and `util`. We then define our `RedisClient` class with a constructor that initializes a new `redis` client and sets up event listeners for connection and errors.

We define methods `isAlive`, `get`, `set`, and `del` which utilize `promisify` from `util` module to convert callback-based functions into Promises. This makes them easier to use asynchronously.

Finally, we create an instance of the `RedisClient` class and export it. This allows us to easily access a shared Redis client throughout our application.

Note: You can replace the hardcoded values for host, port, password, etc., based on your specific Redis configuration.

