To push the `mnpStatus` returned from the API call into the corresponding registration object in your state, you can modify your `checkMNPStatusAPI` function as follows:

```javascript
checkMNPStatusAPI(phoneNo, index) {
  const { registrations } = this.state;

  console.log("mnpphoneno: ", phoneNo);

  var headers = new Headers();
  headers.append("Authorization", "Bearer " + user.getAccessToken());

  fetch(api.checkPortInStatus(user.getProductCode(), phoneNo), {
    headers: headers,
    method: 'GET',
  })
    .then((r) => r.json())
    .then((rJson) => {
      registrations[index].mnpStatus = rJson.status;
      this.setState({ registrations });
    });
}
```

Here, we first destructure the `registrations` state property and store it in a variable called `registrations`. We then make the API call as before, but this time, instead of updating the state directly inside the callback function, we update the `mnpStatus` field of the registration object at the specified index (`index`) in the `registrations` array, and then call `this.setState({ registrations })` to update the entire state with the updated `registrations` array.

